// A superclass is just a normal class that another class inherits from. The difference is in how it is used, not in how it is declared.

// When to use a normal class (no inheritance)

// Use a normal class when it represents a single object and you don't need to share its code with other classes.

// class Book {
//     String title;

//     void read() {
//         System.out.println("Reading " + title);
//     }
// }

// If no other class extends Book, it's simply a normal class.

// When to use a superclass

// Use a superclass when multiple classes have common fields or methods.

// ❌ Without a superclass:

// class Dog {
//     String name;

//     void eat() {
//         System.out.println("Eating");
//     }
// }

// class Cat {
//     String name;

//     void eat() {
//         System.out.println("Eating");
//     }
// }

// Notice that name and eat() are duplicated.

// ✅ With a superclass:

// class Animal {
//     String name;

//     void eat() {
//         System.out.println("Eating");
//     }
// }

// class Dog extends Animal {
//     void bark() {
//         System.out.println("Woof!");
//     }
// }

// class Cat extends Animal {
//     void meow() {
//         System.out.println("Meow!");
//     }
// }

// Now both Dog and Cat inherit name and eat() from Animal.

// How super works

// If the superclass has a constructor, you can call it using super().

// class Animal {
//     String name;

//     Animal(String name) {
//         this.name = name;
//     }
// }

// class Dog extends Animal {

//     Dog(String name) {
//         super(name); // Calls Animal(String name)
//     }
// }
// Rule of thumb

// Use a superclass when:

// ✅ Several classes have the same fields or methods.
// ✅ You want to avoid repeating code.
// ✅ There is an "is-a" relationship.

// Examples:

// Dog is an Animal.
// Car is a Vehicle.
// Student is a Person.

// Don't use a superclass when:

// ❌ Classes are unrelated.
// ❌ You only have one class.
// ❌ You're forcing inheritance just to reuse a little code.

// For example, a Car is not a Driver, so Car extends Driver would be incorrect.

// Quick summary
// Situation	Use
// One independent class	Normal class
// Several classes share common code	Superclass + subclasses
// "Dog is an Animal"	Dog extends Animal
// "Car has an Engine"	Don't use inheritance; use composition instead (Car contains an Engine)

// A good question to ask yourself is: "Can I naturally say 'X is a Y'?" If the answer is yes, inheritance (a superclass) is often appropriate. If the answer is no, composition is usually the better design.

